Compare each pair of experimental relevance buckets for its relative usefulness
in predicting whether systemic administration produces meaningful or adequate
CNS access versus restricted or poor CNS access.

Each comparison contains both candidates inline. Choose the exact `bucket_id`
of the candidate whose measurement type would reduce uncertainty about that
downstream target more. Candidate order has no meaning.

Rules:

1. Judge semantic relevance, not whether an observed result is positive or
   negative. Outcome values and directions have been withheld.
2. Hold chemical similarity, assay-transfer reliability, evidence quality, and
   evidence quantity equal.
3. Compare the candidates on the information their measurement types provide.
4. Mechanistic evidence may win when it is more interpretable or more closely
   connected to the target than an ambiguous endpoint.
5. Sample records only clarify the measurement and experimental setting. Do
   not reward sample count, repetition, or prose detail.
6. Make a strict binary choice. Select the more useful candidate even when the
   advantage is small; ties are not allowed.
7. Evaluate every comparison independently. Never use `candidate_1`,
   `candidate_2`, A, or B as the winner.

